WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.440
Since Negan, played by Jeffrey Dean Morgan,&nbsp;
made his dramatic first appearance on AMC's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.720
The Walking Dead, it has been obvious he's not&nbsp;
a good guy. Good guys don't generally introduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.160
themselves by decapitating two fan-favorite&nbsp;
characters in gruesome fashion. Sure, Jeffrey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.800
Dean Morgan brings a certain rugged charisma&nbsp;
to the role, and his scenery-chewing dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.600
has long been a series highlight, but underneath&nbsp;
it all is a man that has killed out of cruelty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.280
sport, and personal gain. He's also, lest&nbsp;
we forget his many wives, a serial coercive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.440
rapist. His Sanctuary was a brutal, totalitarian&nbsp;
regime governed by a carrot-and-stick system of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.360
physical punishment and monetary reward. The whole&nbsp;
hierarchy was kept aloft by fear. Before Rick's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.960
alliance of survivors took down the Saviors, their&nbsp;
society represented the most extreme extension of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.000
a system based on the flawed premise that "might&nbsp;
makes right." So forgive us if we've always been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.640
a little skeptical of the bat-man's "I did&nbsp;
it because the apocalypse" rationalization.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.640
As The Walking Dead concludes its drawn-out&nbsp;
tenth season, fans have been treated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.440
a dedicated Negan episode that seems to&nbsp;
confirm the worst about Negan's character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.240
The cynics among the show’s viewership may have&nbsp;
already written Negan off as a violent sociopath,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.640
but the latest "bonus" episode of season&nbsp;
10 pretty much confirms for the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.880
us that Negan had issues long before the&nbsp;
dead started climbing out of their graves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.440
and munching on the living. Some people are&nbsp;
just wired wrong, and for Negan, the zombie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.400
apocalypse served as more of an opportunity for&nbsp;
him to indulge his inner psycho, than a trigger.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.960
The episode entitled "Here's Negan," based on&nbsp;
a side story from the comics that was published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.240
in trade paperback under the same name, takes a&nbsp;
look at the character's origin story. The prequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.640
tale reveals what the man was like back when&nbsp;
Lucille (played here by Morgan's actual wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.680
Hilarie Burton) was an actual woman, instead&nbsp;
of a baseball bat wrapped in barbed wire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.480
The verdict isn't great for the big guy in the&nbsp;
leather jacket. While Negan may still get his shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.800
at ultimate redemption on the show — his character&nbsp;
has one of the strangest, most bittersweet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.000
endings in the source material, after all — this&nbsp;
episode proves that he was never a man to admire.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.320
"Here's Negan" shows us a familiar side&nbsp;
of Negan in an unfamiliar environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.560
The former Saviors kingpin had always&nbsp;
relied on the paper-thin justification that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.600
the post-apocalyptic world of The Walking Dead&nbsp;
required a brutal hand to start arranging things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.040
into a shape resembling the lines of civilization&nbsp;
once again. It's all there in the somewhat ironic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.320
name of his group. The Saviors were saving people&nbsp;
from zombies and societal collapse — at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.280
that's what Negan was telling himself and any&nbsp;
unfortunate captive who had to listen. Turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.800
Negan's anger issues and propensity to resort to&nbsp;
violence predate the appearance of the walkers.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.040
In "Here's Negan," the problems all start while&nbsp;
he's enjoying a pleasant night out with his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.280
Lucille. When a belligerent man at the&nbsp;
bar talks over a special song of theirs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.480
Lucille asks the man to lower his voice. The man&nbsp;
proceeds to get in her face instead, and at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.440
moment, Negan loses his cool and attacks him.&nbsp;
Post scuffle, Negan is fired from his job as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.480
gym teacher at the local school, where the man's&nbsp;
kids attend. He is also sued, and Lucille then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.520
has to pay for her aggressor's hospital bills,&nbsp;
because the beating Negan doled out was so severe.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.280
The scene paints a portrait of how&nbsp;
Negan's malicious and vicious ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.440
come from a deep-rooted place in his past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.920
He's always been a little too quick to resort&nbsp;
to violence, which many of us already assumed.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.920
One of the open character questions that the&nbsp;
series will have to grapple with as it approaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:27.440
its endgame is whether or not a man like Negan can&nbsp;
ever truly redeem himself. We've seen plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.400
redemption arcs over the last ten seasons of The&nbsp;
Walking Dead. In general, the stressful conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.040
of the zombie apocalypse allow us to be generous&nbsp;
with characters who have done terrible things to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.400
survive. Negan's a difficult case, though, just&nbsp;
because of the sheer scale of his wrongdoing.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.160
Since his incarceration, Negan has made&nbsp;
some pretty bold attempts to prove that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.120
he's reformed his ways. First, he refused to&nbsp;
escape, even when his cell was left open and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.000
unattended. He also formed a bond with Judith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.640
even risking life and limb to save her&nbsp;
during one of Alexandria's most dire moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.960
Then, he played a crucial (if violent) role&nbsp;
in helping bring down Alpha's Whisperers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:07.920
the most virulent threat to the survivors since&nbsp;
— well – Negan. Anyone who was starting to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.240
around, however, is going to have to rethink&nbsp;
that assessment after watching "Here's Negan."

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.880
Potential spoilers here, but in the comics,&nbsp;
Negan is one of the few characters who actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.040
survives to the final panel. In the final,&nbsp;
flashforward issue that follows an adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.280
Carl Grimes (deceased on the show), we get a&nbsp;
brief glimpse of Negan who has been living a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.320
lonely life in exile — presumably since the&nbsp;
Whisperers arc concluded. The man may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.400
earned his freedom by acting in the interests&nbsp;
of the community, but he never fully redeems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.040
himself. His isolated life is his ultimate&nbsp;
penance for crimes that can never be undone.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.800
We'll have to wait for The Walking Dead's&nbsp;
final season to see if it takes Negan in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.040
similar direction, but the very fact that&nbsp;
the show adapted "Here's Negan" suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.680
that someone on the creative team wanted to&nbsp;
remind us all that Negan's flaws run deep.

